{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ (numbered ? 'field.numbering.title' : 'field.numbering.start_title')|trans }} — {{ field.label }}{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
	{{ include('_topbar.html.twig') }}

	<main class="container page-shell">
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		<div class="d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between mb-3">
			<div>
				<h1 class="h3 mb-0">{{ (numbered ? 'field.numbering.title' : 'field.numbering.start_title')|trans }} — {{ field.label }}</h1>
				{#
					The field's key, because this page is about one column of one
					module and the label is the part that can be changed twice a year.
					The counter is named by the key underneath (§5.10), so it is the
					thing that will still identify this on the day somebody asks why the
					numbers look like that.
				#}
				<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">
					{{ module.label }} · <code>{{ field.key }}</code>
				</p>
			</div>
			<a class="btn btn-link" href="{{ path('field_edit', {module: module.key, field: field.id}) }}">{{ 'field.edit.back_to_field'|trans }}</a>
		</div>

		{#
			The pattern, the number it produces and the counter it comes from are one
			control and not three, which is why they are one component. The pattern is
			handed in as the starting value of a writable prop; everything else the
			component reads for itself, so what it shows after a keystroke and what it
			shows on arrival come from the same code.
		#}
		{#
			The two counts are read here and handed down (XIV-91). They say nothing
			about the pattern — how many records have nothing in this field is true
			whatever somebody types — so reading them per keystroke would be two
			table scans per character for an answer that cannot have changed.

			A field that is already numbered gets them too and ignores them: they
			describe what turning numbering *on* would do, and that decision has
			been made there.

			The default on `sequence` matters now that this page also serves a field
			with no numbering: the option is absent there, and the prop is a string
			that is empty rather than one that is missing.
		#}
		<twig:FieldNumbering module="{{ module.key }}" fieldId="{{ field.id }}"
		                     pattern="{{ field.getOption('sequence', '') }}"
		                     blank="{{ blank }}" held="{{ held }}" />

		{#
			Turning it off, and it is a link to a confirmation rather than a button
			here (§5.4's Remove is the same shape). What it does is not obvious in
			either direction — the numbers stay, the field becomes typeable — so the
			page that says so is the control, and this is the way to it.
		#}
		{% if numbered %}
			<p class="mt-3 mb-0">
				<a class="link-secondary small" href="{{ path('field_numbering_confirm_off', {module: module.key, field: field.id}) }}">
					{{ 'field.numbering.stop_link'|trans }}
				</a>
			</p>
		{% endif %}
	</main>
{% endblock %}
